# G087 — File Explorer

*v2. See `file-explorer.md` for prose: path resolution as the
filesystem's type checker, virtual-filesystem abstraction, root-escape
prevention, dirs-first listing, recursive-size as fold.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault File Pane

[vault-file-pane @vault-root
    IN ORDER TO
        present a file-manager view of the vault
        where path input resolves canonically before any lookup
        and where root escape is structurally impossible

    @explorer <- [fs[load-vault {path: @vault-root}]]

    sync @user-events {
        type-path @path ->
            @resolved <- [fs[resolve
                               {explorer: @explorer, path: @path}]]
            @listing  <- [fs[ls
                               {explorer: @explorer, path: @path}]]
                || -> #fs-error @resistance:reason
            [ui[render-path {resolved: @resolved, listing: @listing}]]

        select @name ->
            @full <- [fs[join {cwd: @explorer:cwd, name: @name}]]
            @stat <- [fs[stat {explorer: @explorer, path: @full}]]
                || -> #fs-error @resistance:reason

            @stat
                where kind is "dir"
                    -> [fs[cd {explorer: @explorer, path: @name}]]
                where kind is "file"
                    -> [ui[open-file {path: @full}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[fs
    new
    cwd             {explorer}
    resolve         {explorer, path}                  ;; -> [String]
    cd              {explorer, path}                  ;; || not-a-dir | not-found
    ls              {explorer, path}                  ;; || not-a-dir | not-found
    mkdir           {explorer, path}                  ;; || already-exists
    touch           {explorer, path, size, mtime}     ;; || not-a-dir
    stat            {explorer, path}                  ;; || not-found
    total-size      {explorer, path}                  ;; -> Int
    join            {cwd, name}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: `resolve` as a normaliser `where` `..` is safe

[fs[resolve {explorer: @e, path: @p}]
    IN ORDER TO
        turn a raw user string into a canonical segment list
        before any lookup or traversal runs
        so that root escape is impossible by construction

    @p
        where absolute
            begins from root
        otherwise
            begins from @e:cwd
    processed segment-by-segment
        where segment is empty or "." -> skip
        where segment is ".." and cwd is at root -> skip (no-op)
        where segment is ".." -> pop one segment
        otherwise -> push segment
]

The normaliser sees `..` before lookup does. Any traversal downstream
receives only well-formed segment lists — the lookup layer never has
to defend against metacharacters.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: listing order as `where` partition then sort

[fs[ls {explorer: @e, path: @p}]
    IN ORDER TO
        list @p's immediate children dirs-first, alphabetical within kind
        so the UI's scan order matches every other file manager in existence

    children of @p
        ordered by
            kind where "dir" sorts before "file",
            name ascending
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @e <- [fs[new]]
    [fs[mkdir {explorer: @e, path: "/home"}]]
    [fs[mkdir {explorer: @e, path: "/home/nathan"}]]
    [fs[mkdir {explorer: @e, path: "/home/nathan/docs"}]]
    [fs[touch {explorer: @e, path: "/home/nathan/docs/a.txt",
                size: 100, mtime: 1000}]]
    [fs[touch {explorer: @e, path: "/home/nathan/notes.md",
                size: 50, mtime: 500}]]

    [fs[cd {explorer: @e, path: "/home/nathan/docs"}]]
    [fs[cd {explorer: @e, path: "../.."}]]
    -> [fs[cwd {explorer: @e}]]                    ;; -> "/home"

    -> [fs[resolve {explorer: @e, path: "/a/./b/../c"}]]
    ;; -> ["a", "c"]

    -> [fs[ls {explorer: @e, path: "/home/nathan"}]]
    ;; -> [{name:"docs", kind:"dir"},
    ;;     {name:"notes.md", kind:"file", size:50}]

    -> [fs[total-size {explorer: @e, path: "/home"}]]   ;; -> 150
]
